WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.856 --> 00:00:03.712
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.320
SEAN: Thank you all for taking
some time out of your day.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.950
We're going to start by looking
at the RMarkdown document.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.180
And how we can use R and
Python together inside

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.780
of that document with
some special new magic

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.180
that's available in the
latest versions of RStudio.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.060
We'll also talk about how
once we finish that document,

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.700
it can be published and
shared in a reproducible way.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.970
So that others can view
it and perhaps, you

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.850
might introduce some
lightweight automation.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.400
We'll look briefly at
how a similar outcome can

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.320
be achieved with
cheaper notebooks using

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.090
shared infrastructure.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.910
And then, we'll talk
about how the model we're

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.300
creating in this
document can actually

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.510
be put into place so
that decisions can

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:51.902
be made based on that model.

00:00:51.902 --> 00:00:53.360
And we'll look at
how you can maybe

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.270
do that with an
interactive app or there is

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.460
something like a RESTful API.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.280
And then finally, we'll
talk about how those APIs

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.800
and applications can
also be developed

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.290
using some of the new options
inside of the RStudio toolset.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.390
So we'll go ahead
and jump in and set

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.020
a little bit of context.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.340
I am using RStudio on a server.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.710
And so that means I'm
going to access RStudio

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.240
through a web browser.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.520
And there's a couple of really
nice benefits to doing this.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.320
The first is that I have more
resources available to me

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.020
than I would on my laptop.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.080
And specifically, if I go
in to start a new session,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.840
you'll see we're taking
advantage of a tool called

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.220
Kubernetes that allows me
to demand to specify what

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.610
resources I want for this work.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.820
Another benefit of
[INAUDIBLE] a server

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:49.480
is that it means everyone
on my team, Lou and myself,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.850
are speaking from
a common playbook.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.800
And in this case,
we're explicitly

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.410
defining our environment
through a Docker image

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.250
where our session
is going to run.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.580
Even if you're not using
Kubernetes or Docker,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.160
by having your work
on a server, everyone

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.050
has a common home,
which helps make

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.160
collaboration a lot easier.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.009
And then finally, because
we're on a server,

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.410
we're a lot closer
to our data, which

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.650
means our work can scale
in an easier fashion.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.478
Now, in a moment I'll be
jumping into RStudio ID.

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:19.270
But while I'm here I
just want to point out

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:20.800
that one of the
things we've done

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.350
in the last year for
multilingual teams

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.410
is added the ability
for other editing tools

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.300
to be accessed from
this same interface.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.180
So it's almost like
a Workbench, where

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.490
you can pick up the right
tool from that Workbench

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:38.350
regardless of what language
your current project demands.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.170
And that means for multilingual
data science teams,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.030
everyone has all
those benefits I just

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.800
described that the
common playbook,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.010
regardless of what
editor they're using.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.750
And for IT, it's really nice
to have this common front

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.400
door or these
different environments

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.840
are going to be accessed.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.010
So we'll go ahead and
jump into RStudio.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.780
And at this point,
I want to point out

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.440
that everything you're going
to see inside of RStudio IDE

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.590
even though I'm using
the RStudio server

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.580
is going to be available in
any flavor of the RStudio IDE.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.280
So whether you're using the
open-source desktop or RStudio

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.280
Server open source or the
professional product like I am,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.100
you'll have access to
the interoperability

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.330
that we're about to get through.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.810
So to set the stage
just a little bit more,

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.830
I'm doing my work here inside
of an RMarkdown document.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.170
If you haven't seen
RMarkdown before,

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.530
it's basically a format
where I can combine pros.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:36.730
So what I'm thinking along with
my code and then the output

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.600
of my code.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.880
But what's new in the
latest version of RStudio

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.520
is something that we're
calling the visual editor.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.640
And what that means is that even
though I'm editing an RMarkdown

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.180
document, on the fly I'm
getting a live rendering of what

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.750
this document looks like.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.590
And so that means I can do
things a little bit easier

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:55.310
than I used to.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.220
So, for example, if I
needed to put in a table,

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.750
I could insert a
table really quickly.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.770
I can add Markdown
to that table and it

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.460
will be rendered on-demand.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:10.100
I can even do things like
insert citations or emojis.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.180
And so I can use all these
different tools inside

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.890
of the visual editor.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.170
And whatever I'm doing
is going to show up

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.600
as a real-time preview.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.820
And the reason why I
think this is so important

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.260
is that regardless of what
language you're working from,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:31.130
ultimately, a data scientist's
key job is to communicate.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.200
And so being able
to write effectively

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.210
in a tool that
supports really rich

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:40.070
technical communication is vital
for any type of data scientist.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.930
And I found that
this visual editor

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.970
makes things that
used to be challenging

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.570
for that type of technical
communication really easy.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.700
One of my favorites
is I can go in here

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.573
and actually insert an image.

00:04:51.573 --> 00:04:53.240
I don't have to worry
about figuring out

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:54.990
where that image lives on disk.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.120
And I can even resize
it interactively.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.330
So we hope that regardless
of what language,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:04.888
this visual editor is going to
give you a head start as you're

00:05:04.888 --> 00:05:06.930
writing down what you're
thinking and documenting

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.100
your process.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.240
And it really combines the best
worlds of a Jupyter Notebook

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.090
[INAUDIBLE] IDE and RMarkdown.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.830
And if you haven't
seen this before,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.440
inside the latest version of
RStudio, all you need to do

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.330
is click this button in the
upper right hand corner.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.380
And what that then does
is flip us back and forth

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.140
between the plain
text source code that

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.140
is still available here
perfect for version control

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.340
and that pre-rendered
view of the document.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:34.840
All right.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.580
So that's where
we're doing our work.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.500
It is inside of
this visual editor.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.710
What are we going to do?

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.270
We have a made-up
data science exercise.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.550
And so like most data
science exercises,

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.750
we're going to start by
pulling in some data.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.080
And this data is
coming from a database.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.630
And again, something that
we're excited about regardless

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.200
of what language you use is
the ability within RStudio

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.590
to seamlessly manage
connections to databases.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:02.460
And so here I have a connection
to a database called content.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.660
There's a number of different
tables inside of it.

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.060
And without switching to
a different sequel editor,

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.380
I can really easily preview the
schema of this database table

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.240
and even preview some of
the records if I want.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.920
So I have this database
and I'm starting out

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.680
by writing some code
that's going to operate

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.110
within the database to
do some basic transforms.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.340
And so if you haven't seen
this before, using dplyr,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.470
you can write R code.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.852
Then that R code actually
gets executed as a sequel.

00:06:32.852 --> 00:06:34.310
And so that's what's
going on here.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.570
So we're creating some
data that we're then

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.200
going to do an analysis on.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.220
So let's take a quick
look at that data.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.520
Essentially, we have
spatial-temporal data.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.440
And so we're looking
at bike-share data

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.500
from the D.C. area.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.040
So those bike shares,
if you've ever

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.800
been walking around the city
and you want to rent a bike,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.295
you can usually find a
station with bikes lined up

00:06:57.295 --> 00:06:58.670
and then you pay
some money to be

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:00.110
able to ride the bike around.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:02.630
And so the data that
we have is how many

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.240
bikes were available
at any given

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.440
station at any point in time.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.400
So we have this time series
of bikes that are available,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.750
then we also have a
spatial component of where

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.620
those bikes were located.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.050
So it's a pretty cool data set.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:21.080
And what we want to
try to do is forecast

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.180
into the future
for a given station

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.040
how many bikes
might be available.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.190
And say, if you've
commute home at 5:00 PM,

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:31.190
is there going to be a
bike there that I can take?

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:33.970
And so we'll get started
by doing that in R.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.720
And the first thing
that we're going to do

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.445
is create a test and
training data set.

00:07:38.445 --> 00:07:41.310
And because we're working
with time-series data,

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:43.310
it's really important
that we don't accidentally

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:45.720
use the future to
predict the past.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.490
And so, in R we've created,
a test and training data set,

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.010
where the training data
set sequentially occurs

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:54.830
before the test data set.

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.900
And now, that we have
these two things,

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.630
we can go ahead and
build our model.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.200
And I'm using a new R package
or an ecosystem of R packages

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.330
called tidy models.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.010
There's a lot of
great resources online

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.320
if you want to dive
into modeling an R.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.140
But essentially, what
this set of packages

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:15.990
is allowing us to do is
pre-process our data.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.830
And so, we have here
latitude and longitude.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.510
That's the spatial element.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:22.370
We have the date and time.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.410
That's the temporal element.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.660
But we've used some
preprocessing tricks

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.150
to create a factor for
the day of the week

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:31.700
and also add some information
about whether a given

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.169
day was a holiday.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:35.960
So you have a nice rich
data set to do modeling on.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.090
And then, the first
thing that we might do

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.299
is create a model in R.

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:45.380
And so to do that, again, within
this tidy model's ecosystem

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.870
and essentially
creating a workflow.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:51.680
That's going to take our
model preprocessing along

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.470
with this gradient
boosted engine

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:57.510
and fit a simple
regression model.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.700
So we can go ahead and do that.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.200
And if we look at
the results here,

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.530
we have a predicted
number of bikes

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.050
along with the actual
number of bikes, kind

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.530
of what we'd expect.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.010
And we can evaluate
how our prediction did.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:16.520
So one thing that we can do
for that quick evaluation

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.130
is plot the prediction
versus the reality.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:23.420
And if our model is really
good, all of our data points

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.000
would fall along
this y equals x line,

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.020
where predictions match reality.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:31.580
You can see, we have quite
the variance from that line.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:34.670
We could look at the R
squared value of our model

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:38.630
and see that it's not very good.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:42.308
That's why I'm paid to give
webinars and not fit models

00:09:42.308 --> 00:09:43.850
but this hopefully,
gives you a sense

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:47.990
of what your workflow might
start to look like an R. We can

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:51.350
even because this is
a tree-based model.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.390
Look at feature
importance, and so you

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.490
can see that our
model is emphasizing

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.610
the location of our
bike share station

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.040
and not putting as much weight
on some of those factors

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:02.690
that we create.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:06.817
So we have this iffy model in
R. What do we want to do next?

00:10:06.817 --> 00:10:08.650
Well, we could try a
lot of different things

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:09.980
to improve our model.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:13.600
One thing we might want
to do is try something

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.280
from the Python ecosystem.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.410
And so that's where the
heart of this webinar

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.180
begins which is,
how are we going

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:24.220
to interoperate Python and R
inside of the same context?

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.960
Well, the first
thing that we'll do

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.030
is create a Python code chunk.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.040
And inside of that
Python code chunk,

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:33.070
one of the things that we're
going to do right after that

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.110
is import some packages.

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:39.690
So go ahead and add the code
to bring in these packages.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.240
And we can do a quick
sanity check here

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:44.880
to run the code
chunk and it appears

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.065
that we have these packages
loaded inside of Python.

00:10:48.065 --> 00:10:50.190
Now, I can already see in
the chat that many of you

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.500
are asking how is
Python managed?

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.120
Where are these
packages coming from?

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.020
Those are really
great questions.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.560
So in the latest
version of RStudio,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.860
you can use a project
or global option

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.170
to specify what Python
interpreter should

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.180
be used in the
context where you're

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.440
mixing R and Python together.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.680
And so for this project, I've
created a virtual environment

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.370
and I have selected
and told RStudio to use

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:15.780
that virtual environment.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.850
They can see that
RStudio is actually

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:22.740
aware of the multitude
of Python installs

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.400
that are available
on this server.

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.880
And that's one of those
benefits of working on a server

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.310
is that if Lou and I were
collaborating together

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.860
we'd have this common
understanding of what Python

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.520
installations are available.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.960
So that's where the Python
engine is coming from.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.650
The Python packages themselves
are coming from a tool called

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.510
RStudio package manager.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:47.260
So in our case, our
server is not online.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:48.660
Here, we have some
sensitive data

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:49.910
that lives in our environment.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.310
So we're not able to go
reach out to the internet.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:55.570
So instead package manager
acts as this intermediary,

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:59.890
where we can install Python
packages from a specifically

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:03.010
governed mirror of PyPI.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.200
And so that's what
you're looking at here--

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.930
I can search for packages,
I can see information

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.940
about those packages,
how to install them,

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:11.560
what they depend on.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.885
But something really
special about this mirror

00:12:13.885 --> 00:12:16.390
is that it has a
safety net that allows

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.358
me to time travel as well.

00:12:18.358 --> 00:12:19.900
And so if I ever
got into a situation

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:22.060
where my Python
environment wasn't working,

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.160
I could go backward
or forward in time

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:28.750
to reinstall packages from a
specific point in the past.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.510
So that's where these
packages are coming from.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:33.870
Let's go ahead and
start writing some code.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.230
And this is where I think
things get really magical.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:40.170
Because within this
Python code chunk,

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.960
inside of the
RMarkdown document,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.660
I actually have
access to everything

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.780
that I've done up
until this point in R.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:50.580
And you can actually
see the idea here

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:53.550
is helping me autocomplete
some of the objects that

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:58.480
are attributes of this special
R object that are available.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:12:59.770
And so what does that mean?

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:01.740
Well, I'm going to
cheat a little bit

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:06.900
and grab some code that
I've written ahead of time.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.340
We'll just put this
