00:17:22.609 --> 00:17:25.210
our recommendation is to
publish to RStudio Connect.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:26.869
And so I'll go
ahead and do that.

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:30.380
You can see the ID
identifies my dependencies.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:32.420
And what happens
when I click Publish

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:35.630
is that a reproducible
unit is created here

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:38.150
that contains not
only my code, but also

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.790
things like the version of
R, the version of Python,

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:44.090
the different packages that
I need to reliably reproduce

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:45.570
this document.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.240
And so I'll go and
show you the end

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:50.240
result here on
RStudio Connect, which

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.550
is that same rendered notebook.

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:55.190
So we have kind of
all the information

00:17:55.190 --> 00:18:00.920
that I have been working up to
this point, but in the context

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:02.970
where I can easily
share it with others.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.910
So I can specify who
should be able to see this.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:09.020
And because it's that
reproducible unit,

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.500
at any point, I can reliably
refresh this document

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.380
to regenerate the results,
or I could do something

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:16.890
like set up a schedule.

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:19.010
So my data might
change over time,

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:23.090
but I want this notebook to
render on a regular basis.

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:26.270
And I can set all of that up
because the dependencies are

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:30.545
intertwined in a reproducible
unit with this document.

00:18:30.545 --> 00:18:31.920
Now you might be
thinking, that's

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:36.600
great if you're RStudio user,
which probably most of you are.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:39.090
But what about my colleagues
on the Python side?

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:42.000
Well, the same option is
available for sharing your work

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.970
early and often if you're
coming from Python.

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:48.540
And so I want to quickly show
you what that looks like.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:51.840
In the RStudio IDE we click
that blue Publish button.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.790
And inside of Jupyter
Notebooks, you

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.280
can click that blue
Publish button as well.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.320
But I want to show you
a slightly different way

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:01.440
that you can quickly and
easily share your content.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:05.250
And that's to import content
from a system like Git.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.015
So what I have here
is a Git repository.

00:19:09.015 --> 00:19:12.510
And that Git repository
contains all of the example code

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.130
we've been talking about today.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:15.720
And it's a public
