00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.970
Here, they just have a
really clean HTML document

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.350
that they can read.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:09.142
And a data scientist
can specify who

00:20:09.142 --> 00:20:10.600
should be able to
see this content,

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.510
but also do those
same things I was

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:14.850
talking about for
scheduling or re-rendering

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.450
the content on demand.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:18.580
So we have our notebooks.

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.230
We've shared them
early and often.

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:21.130
That's great.

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:22.900
We've gotten this
domain feedback.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.120
And maybe we iterate
a couple of times

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:27.730
and really improve that model.

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:29.180
What do we do with that?

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:31.870
How do we ensure that the
model is actually being

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:34.600
used to generate decisions?

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:37.930
That's kind of the key task
of the data science team.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:41.590
Well, there's two ways that we
think about this at RStudio.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:47.290
One is to influence how decision
makers are making decisions

00:20:47.290 --> 00:20:48.700
through your model.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:50.380
And a great way to
do that is through

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:52.270
interactive applications.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:55.750
So many of you might be familiar
with tools like Shiny, which

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:59.350
allow you to do
that in R. But we've

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:02.830
worked hard in the last year to
ensure that multilingual teams

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:04.850
have that same capability.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.140
So what you're looking at
here is a Dash application,

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:12.070
which is an interactive
application written in Python.

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:15.130
And what this application
is going to allow us to do

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:18.890
is help stakeholders
understand our model.

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:21.220
So they can come
in here and click

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:23.320
through different
stations and see

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:26.180
the forecast and the
location of the station.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.550
So it's a pretty simple app.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:29.830
But hopefully, it kind
of gets your wheels

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:32.890
turning that even if you're
a Python data scientist

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.020
or you work with
Python data scientists,

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:37.930
they have the same ability
to impact decisions

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:39.760
by creating interactive content.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.550
And we see examples of that
with Dash, with Streamlit,

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:46.120
with Bokeh, with a wide
variety of interactive Python

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:50.080
frameworks that are supported
through the RStudio stack.

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:52.390
So that's how you might
go about influencing

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:54.467
a person with your model.

00:21:54.467 --> 00:21:56.050
But what if you need
to use your model

00:21:56.050 --> 00:21:58.870
to make a whole bunch
of automated decisions,

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:04.060
or if you need to influence
not a person, but a service?

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:06.550
One way that's common
to solve that problem

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:09.390
is by creating an API.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:13.030
And again, there's options for
doing this in R and Python.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.020
So on the R side, we
have tools like Plumber.

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:19.350
On the Python side, we
have tools like Flask.

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:22.830
And both of these can
be shared just as easily

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:24.330
through RStudio Connect.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:27.690
And so I'll just quickly show
you what that API might entail.

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:30.480
Again, we have all
of the same controls.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.290
And so we can look at the
logs of this Plumber API.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.683
We could do things like scale.

00:22:36.683 --> 00:22:38.850
If we know we're going to
have thousands of requests

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.650
to this API at the
same time, we can

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:44.170
specify how we want the system
to handle those requests.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.630
But at the end of the day,
the idea is pretty simple.

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.835
It's that anyone can
come in and place

00:22:50.835 --> 00:22:53.080
a parameter of your model--

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.050
In this case, we're
specifying the station

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:56.850
that we want to make
a prediction for

00:22:56.850 --> 00:23:00.480
and the time horizon that we
want to make a prediction for--

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.870
and then those inputs
are passed to our model

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.830
and the results are returned.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:08.740
So here, we have the
results of our forecast.

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:12.570
But as you can see, that
passing of inputs and outputs

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.250
is done in a way that
machines can understand.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:18.000
So here, we have
the output in JSON.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.550
And just above that,
we have the request

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:25.530
that our interactive exploration
of this API would generate.

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.680
So other systems or services
or software engineers

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:32.340
can take advantage of
your model at scale.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:32.840
All right.

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:34.830
So we've covered
quite a bit of ground.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.530
We've talked about how to make
these models through notebooks.

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.740
We've talked about
the different ways

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:41.660
that you can
enhance those models

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:44.090
and put them into production,
but didn't actually show

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:48.110
you the code for either that
Dash application or this API.

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:50.420
So that's the last thing
that I wanted to do

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:52.880
is talk a little bit about
how you can get started

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.665
writing in this type of code.

00:23:54.665 --> 00:23:57.620
And so if I go back
to the RStudio IDE,

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.320
if you're an R user,
my recommendation

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.810
is to just click
New File, and you'll

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:04.910
see a bunch of
options, two of which

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:08.300
are Shiny web applications
and Plumber APIs.

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:10.490
So those are going to get
you started with creating

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:13.280
either web apps or APIs.

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:17.030
If you're a Python user,
inside of the RStudio

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:20.480
stack we looked at
Jupyter Notebooks.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:22.790
But if you want to do
this type of coding

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.550
for APIs or applications,
you're probably

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:27.770
going to need a little
