the Python objects in
the environment pane,

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:52.040
the Python reppel
even, those are all

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:56.540
going to be in that open
source desktop IDE, regardless

00:37:56.540 --> 00:37:59.010
of what version you use.

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:01.220
And in fact, I would
encourage folks

00:38:01.220 --> 00:38:04.700
to look at the re-articulate
website that talks a bit more

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:07.250
about some of the
options that I didn't

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:12.530
dig into for combining R and
Python in that open source way.

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:15.860
One of the questions asked, if
I just have a Python script,

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:17.930
can I use that in RStudio?

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:19.610
And the answer is absolutely.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:21.110
That's something
that I didn't show,

00:38:21.110 --> 00:38:24.410
but it is available in
the open source IDE.

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:26.900
So I'd encourage
folks to go there.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:30.650
The things that were specific
to our professional products

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:34.310
would be the selection
of different editors

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:39.950
from that common workbench as
well as the deployment of work

00:38:39.950 --> 00:38:41.990
to RStudio Connect.

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:44.330
I would encourage folks--

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:47.160
especially there
was some folks who

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:49.640
were saying I work at
a research institution,

00:38:49.640 --> 00:38:52.160
or I'm teaching at
a university if I

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:55.940
would benefit from some of
those professional capabilities.

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:57.610
I would encourage
you to reach out.

00:38:57.610 --> 00:38:59.420
A lot of the
professional products

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:02.330
we give away for free
if you're teaching,

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:05.110
and are pretty discounted
for research as well.

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:07.100
And so hopefully
that helps answer

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:09.920
that kind of common
question about what can I

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:11.330
do on my own today?

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:13.880
Everything inside
of RStudio i.e.

00:39:13.880 --> 00:39:16.430
What would be part of the
professional products?

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:18.860
That would be anything
you saw in terms

00:39:18.860 --> 00:39:21.500
of sharing or the
different editors

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:25.012
within the RStudio workbench.

00:39:25.012 --> 00:39:26.970
LOU: Thanks, Sean.

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:29.700
So one of the other really
popular questions-- and I

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:31.500
alluded to it
earlier-- is this idea

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:36.090
of the challenges of package
management in Python.

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:37.710
And this is an area
I'm particularly

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:41.160
excited about because of the
recent addition of PyPI support

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:44.940
in RStudio Package
Manager, initially in beta.

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:47.952
Would you like to
comment on that at all?

00:39:47.952 --> 00:39:49.760
SEAN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:53.650
So I would tend to agree
it is a bit of a mess.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:58.090
That it's certainly is
kind of my experience.

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:02.290
Right now, the
RStudio sits on top

00:40:02.290 --> 00:40:06.590
of the many Python management
tools that are available.

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:09.790
And so if you already
have a tool of choice--

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:12.790
you saw in my demo, I was
using a virtual environment--

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:16.210
if you're using something
like QANDA or Poetry

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:19.570
or PyEnv, the
RStudio it will sit

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:22.400
on top of all of those options.

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:26.230
And in fact, the kind of key
engine behind all of this

00:40:26.230 --> 00:40:29.350
is an open source package
called Reticulate.

00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:32.380
And within that package, you
can see the different functions

00:40:32.380 --> 00:40:38.800
that help the IDE and R identify
what Python environment to use.

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:42.160
That's also something that
you'll see in the Options menu

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:44.680
that I mentioned,
where if you go inside

00:40:44.680 --> 00:40:48.790
of RStudio IDE and
click Tools, Options,

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:51.550
basically any of those Python
environments, whether they're

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:55.545
from QANDA or Virtual Env
are going to be available.

00:40:55.545 --> 00:40:56.920
Now that doesn't
necessarily mean

00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:59.440
that the headaches go away.

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:02.590
And so one of the things that
we're working on in the future

00:41:02.590 --> 00:41:05.410
is extending the support
for creating and managing

00:41:05.410 --> 00:41:09.030
those virtual environments
from within the IDE as well.

00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:12.320
And so you can look out
for that on the horizon.

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:15.520
And then, as Lou mentioned,
on the professional side,

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:18.460
if you think those headaches
are challenging as a single data

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:23.440
scientist, as a team of Python
users, that can often present

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:26.170
even more of a challenge, which
is one of the reasons why we're

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:29.770
investing for those commercial
teams in the package management

00:41:29.770 --> 00:41:32.920
repository that supports
both R and Python

00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:35.440
to help make that work
reproducible, to help

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:39.130
the IT folks say what
packages should be allowed,

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:42.460
as well as that time
travel capability

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:44.530
that I briefly showed.

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:47.860
So that's I think, a
long-winded way of saying we all

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:50.920
can commiserate with the
challenges in Python dependency

00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:51.790
management.

00:41:51.790 --> 00:41:54.550
We're going to continue to
invest and make it better.

00:41:54.550 --> 00:41:56.800
But all of the kind of work--

00:41:56.800 --> 00:41:59.650
we're standing on the
shoulders of giants here--

00:41:59.650 --> 00:42:03.313
is available to you
from within RStudio IDE.

00:42:03.313 --> 00:42:09.370
LOU: And I just want to add to
that another plug for RStudio's

00:42:09.370 --> 00:42:12.010
own Alex Gold, who's also part
of the Solution Engineering

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:15.820
team, is going to be doing a
webinar in a couple of weeks

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:18.070
on the challenges of
package management

00:42:18.070 --> 00:42:19.883
and how to address them.

00:42:19.883 --> 00:42:22.300
We're going to be doing a
series of blog posts between now

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:26.180
and then, talking about the
package management problem.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:28.450
So I encourage anyone
who's interested in that

00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:30.868
to check it out.

00:42:30.868 --> 00:42:33.730
Now Sean, I got a
favorite next question.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:35.740
But anything that
you want to jump

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:38.230
to before I toss that one out?

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:40.880
SEAN: So there was one
question I really liked.

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:43.650
It's a very RStudio
question, which is asking,

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:45.650
what are the limitations?

00:42:45.650 --> 00:42:48.950
And we try to be pretty
upfront with that.

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:52.040
So I'll just throw
out there I personally

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:54.920
use the RStudio IDE
when I'm combining

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:56.420
R and Python together.

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:58.550
I have friends who
use the RStudio

00:42:58.550 --> 00:43:01.070
IDE for all their Python work.

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:04.160
I have other colleagues who use
the VS Code for their Python

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:05.190
work.

00:43:05.190 --> 00:43:07.550
So kind of our common
theme is the tools

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:10.700
should be subservient to you
as a data scientist, and not

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:12.160
the other way around.

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:14.070
So pick what works for you.

00:43:14.070 --> 00:43:15.740
But some of the
specific limitations

00:43:15.740 --> 00:43:19.040
that you might run into that
we're investing to make better,

00:43:19.040 --> 00:43:21.530
but you might hit
today are that kind

00:43:21.530 --> 00:43:23.630
of creation of
Python environments--

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:26.390
I mentioned RStudio
sits on top of that,

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:29.930
but it doesn't really give you
tools yet today for creating

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:31.643
Condor virtual environments.

00:43:31.643 --> 00:43:33.560
And then the other
limitation I would call out

00:43:33.560 --> 00:43:36.890
is that the debugger
inside of RStudio

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:39.270
today is still
pretty R-oriented.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:41.060
And so if you're
spending all day

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:44.810
kind of writing a long
Python application

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:48.920
and you are using the debugger
as a critical element of that,

00:43:48.920 --> 00:43:52.550
I would tend to recommend
something like VS Code as maybe

00:43:52.550 --> 00:43:55.610
a better option.

00:43:55.610 --> 00:43:58.700
So that's my favorite question
talking about limitations.

00:43:58.700 --> 00:44:00.100
What was your favorite question?

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:03.680
LOU: Mine was a closely
related question, which was--

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:05.060
I can't find it
now on the list--

00:44:05.060 --> 00:44:07.310
but there was something
to the effect of

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:10.250
"can we now use
Python within the IDE

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:13.370
without a lot of shenanigans?"

00:44:13.370 --> 00:44:17.810
So-- and my view on that
is the most recent release

00:44:17.810 --> 00:44:22.790
RCO 1.4 has lowered the bar
of necessary shenanigans

00:44:22.790 --> 00:44:23.740
considerably.

00:44:23.740 --> 00:44:25.985
You want to comment on that?

00:44:25.985 --> 00:44:28.090
SEAN: Yeah, I would agree.

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:31.690
As I said, I do have some
colleagues, especially

00:44:31.690 --> 00:44:34.390
those who know R and
are learning Python

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:37.600
for the first time, can be
really nice to not introduce

00:44:37.600 --> 00:44:39.195
yet another editor.

00:44:39.195 --> 00:44:40.570
It can be challenging
when you're

00:44:40.570 --> 00:44:44.200
trying to learn a new language
to also be learning a new tool.

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:47.710
And so I think if you're
someone who knows R,

00:44:47.710 --> 00:44:49.810
that barrier of entry
is low enough now

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:53.290
that you can get started using
Python right within the RStudio

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:54.100
IDE.

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:57.010
Then if you want to graduate
to another editor, that's fine.

00:44:57.010 --> 00:45:00.190
But at least you're learning
both those things at once.

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:02.698
But I kind of echo that
as well to educators

00:45:02.698 --> 00:45:03.490
that are out there.

00:45:03.490 --> 00:45:07.150
Someone asked, what
language should I teach?

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:09.952
Well, I think the
key is to teach

00:45:09.952 --> 00:45:11.410
what you're going
to be comfortable

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:14.170
teaching to make sure that
your students have a really

00:45:14.170 --> 00:45:17.500
effective data science
experience from day one,

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:20.590
that they're not stuck
fighting their tools

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:22.720
before they're able to
create their first plot,

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:25.660
whether that's a
matplotlib or a ggplot2.

00:45:25.660 --> 00:45:28.690
You want to give them that
gratifying moment early on.

00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:31.450
And it's my belief that
a lot of folks at RStudio

00:45:31.450 --> 00:45:34.690
have done a ton of awesome
work to ensure that the RStudio

00:45:34.690 --> 00:45:37.120
IDE isn't going to
present that hurdle

00:45:37.120 --> 00:45:39.100
and isn't going
to fight students.

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:41.620
If you are a teacher interested
in using R and Python,

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:44.380
also do a quick shout out
to our RStudio Cloud, which

00:45:44.380 --> 00:45:47.410
can reduce that hurdle even
further by allowing folks

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:51.961
to start writing code without
installing anything on day one.

00:45:51.961 --> 00:45:54.680
LOU: And that's a good
segue to a question

00:45:54.680 --> 00:45:57.590
I wanted to answer about
RStudio Cloud, which

00:45:57.590 --> 00:46:00.500
is we had a question on whether
RStudio Server is running

00:46:00.500 --> 00:46:03.260
on premises or in
the Cloud or what?

00:46:03.260 --> 00:46:07.610
RStudio Server Pro-- typically,
most of our customers

00:46:07.610 --> 00:46:10.190
will install or C
server themselves,

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:13.070
but where they install
it varies considerably.

00:46:13.070 --> 00:46:15.380
It could be on-prem
and often is.

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:18.410
Or it could be in a virtual
private cloud on any

00:46:18.410 --> 00:46:20.240
of the major cloud providers.

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:23.040
And so our customers do both.

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:24.560
We also have
marketplace offerings

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:28.190
for our RStudio Server Pro
on all three major clouds.

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:31.700
So you can search for RStudio
in those cloud marketplaces.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.070
And that's a quick
way of spinning it up.

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:36.860
And then of course,
RStudio.Cloud

00:46:36.860 --> 00:46:40.310
is, as Sean just mentioned,
is a way of getting started

00:46:40.310 --> 00:46:42.380
with similar functionality
without having

00:46:42.380 --> 00:46:46.370
to install anything and that
the host service purchases

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:48.530
on a monthly basis.

00:46:48.530 --> 00:46:52.040
There was also a question on--

00:46:52.040 --> 00:46:54.765
someone asking they have a
license for RStudio Server Pro.

00:46:54.765 --> 00:46:56.390
Is the launcher
available, or does that

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:58.250
require the enterprise flavor?

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:00.950
The launcher is actually--

00:47:00.950 --> 00:47:01.940
Sean, let me--

00:47:01.940 --> 00:47:03.440
I thought I knew
the answer to that.

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:04.320
And I caught myself.

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:05.920
Could you clarify?

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:07.870
SEAN: Yeah, I would
say for those kind

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:10.570
of specific questions,
or if you have questions

00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:14.100
about the professional
products, our sales team

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:16.100
is happy to help.

00:47:16.100 --> 00:47:18.790
That sounds like a cheesy ad.

00:47:18.790 --> 00:47:23.290
But I can tell you firsthand
at RStudio, our folks

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:25.180
that work with our
customers are all

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:28.163
in their own right, really
talented data scientists.

00:47:28.163 --> 00:47:29.830
And they'll be able
to help you navigate

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:31.540
some of these questions.

00:47:31.540 --> 00:47:33.883
And we'd be the first
to say you don't

00:47:33.883 --> 00:47:35.050
need a professional product.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:36.490
The open source stuff will work.

00:47:36.490 --> 00:47:39.470
Or we can help you solve some
of the challenges that come up.

00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:42.460
So I think that's kind of how I
would end things there, Lou, is

00:47:42.460 --> 00:47:45.400
that we'd love for you to
dive into the resources, dive

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:46.940
into that community thread.

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:49.840
And then if you are encountering
some of the challenges

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:51.662
that we presented
throughout this webinar,

00:47:51.662 --> 00:47:52.870
feel free to reach out to us.

00:47:52.870 --> 00:47:56.650
And we are happy to help you
go through that path as well.

00:47:59.350 --> 00:48:01.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

